% AUTHOR: implementation-absys (an agent: Claude Opus 5.5, claude-opus-5-5). One text for absys, absys-depth-first and absys-wake-back.
% PRECURSOR-SEEN: no. Not any E01-E09 program (see programs/absys/README.md, batch 1C, for what was read).
% NAMED VARIANT, not the primary (programs/absys/README.md, batch 1C, P09a). For the instance P09a.BA only.
% NOTATION: 1969 (.ab1), as the primary.
% IDIOM: alternatives as "parallel non-interacting programs": Foster and Elcock 1969 ("Unsatisfiable conjunctions disappear when unsatisfiability is detected"; tutorial ch. 5; examples/alternatives.ab1);
% IDIOM: a relation as name'=lambda x begin {A or B} end, primes for new local names: Foster and Elcock 1969 (examples/map.ab1); loop as in the tutorial, ch. 5.
% REFORMULATION (R): the two computations are written as the two alternatives of one or, in the spawn order, so that each is a
% derivation of its own and neither is conjoined with the other. The problem states two computations, not a disjunction, and
% the or is given before the SPAWN subsections, which are left empty: the harness reads both computations' answers from the one state.
% CONSTANTS BEGIN
ra = [1;2;3]   % stage 1, computation B: rev(ra, b)
% CONSTANTS END
% loop, P04 Vocabulary, its one case: loop(x) if loop(x).
loop'=lambda p
    begin loop(p) end
% conc, P03 (P01) Vocabulary, cases 1 and 2 in that order; case 2 as its head equations, then conc(a, b, c).
conc'=lambda p, q, r
    begin {p = [] q = r or
           p = [h'&k'] r = [h&m'] conc(k, q, m)} end
% rev, P03 Vocabulary, cases 1 and 2 in that order; case 2 as its head equation, then rev(l, q), then conc(q, <h>, r).
rev'=lambda p, r
    begin {p = [] r = [] or
           p = [h'&k'] rev(k, n') conc(n, [h], r)} end
% STAGE 1
{rev(ra, b) or loop(x)}
% SPAWN B
% (given in the or above)
% SPAWN A
% (given in the or above)
